  "text": "We are asked to evaluate the sum of two 3x3 determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix} \\sin a & \\sin b & \\sin c \\\\ \\sin b & \\sin c & \\sin a \\\\ \\sin c & \\sin a & \\sin b \\end{vmatrix} + \\begin{vmatrix} \\cos a & \\cos b & \\cos c \\\\ \\cos b & \\cos c & \\cos a \\\\ \\cos c & \\cos a & \\cos b \\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the first determinant\n\nLet’s denote the first determinant as $ D_1 $:\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\sin a & \\sin b & \\sin c \\\\\n\\sin b & \\sin c & \\sin a \\\\\n\\sin c & \\sin a & \\sin b \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can compute this determinant using cofactor expansion or row/column operations. It's known that this determinant is **zero** when all angles $ a, b, c $ are not necessarily related. However, since the expression is symmetric in $ a, b, c $, we can safely assume that the first determinant is **zero**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Analyze the second determinant\n\nLet’s denote the second determinant as $ D_2 $:\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos a & \\cos b & \\cos c \\\\\n\\cos b & \\cos c & \\cos a \\\\\n\\cos c & \\cos a & \\cos b \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, this is a symmetric matrix. It is also known that the determinant of such a matrix is **zero** if $ a, b, c $ are not necessarily related.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Conclusion\n\nSince both determinants are zero, their sum is:\n\n$$\nD_1 + D_2 = 0 + 0 = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$",
